Got it 👍 you’re asking about updating a list while it’s being processed.
This is a tricky area in Java because of ConcurrentModificationException that happens if you modify a list while iterating.

Here are the safe ways 👇

1. Use CopyOnWriteArrayList

This list is designed for concurrent read/write.

Every modification creates a new copy of the list internally.

Best for more reads, fewer writes.

import java.util.concurrent.CopyOnWriteArrayList;

public class Example {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CopyOnWriteArrayList<String> list = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();
        list.add("A");
        list.add("B");

        for (String s : list) {
            if (s.equals("A")) {
                list.add("C"); // Safe update during iteration
            }
        }

        System.out.println(list); // [A, B, C]
    }
}

2. Use Iterator with iterator.remove()

Works only for removal, not for adding new elements.

import java.util.*;

public class Example {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> list = new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList("A", "B", "C"));

        Iterator<String> it = list.iterator();
        while (it.hasNext()) {
            String s = it.next();
            if (s.equals("B")) {
                it.remove(); // Safe remove
            }
        }

        System.out.println(list); // [A, C]
    }
}

3. Collect updates separately, then apply

Instead of modifying inside the loop, collect what to add/remove in a temp list.

Apply changes after the iteration.

List<String> list = new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList("A", "B", "C"));
List<String> toAdd = new ArrayList<>();

for (String s : list) {
    if (s.equals("A")) {
        toAdd.add("D");
    }
}
list.addAll(toAdd);

System.out.println(list); // [A, B, C, D]

4. Use Streams with mapping

If you want to transform elements, better use streams.

List<String> updated = list.stream()
                           .map(s -> s.equals("A") ? "Z" : s)
                           .toList();


✅ 5. Collections.synchronizedList()

Wraps a normal List with synchronized methods.

import java.util.*;

public class Example {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> list = Collections.synchronizedList(new ArrayList<>());

        // Safe add/remove across threads
        list.add("A");
        list.add("B");

        // When iterating, must synchronize manually
        synchronized (list) {
            for (String s : list) {
                System.out.println(s);
            }
        }
    }
}


⚠️ Drawback: Single lock → may reduce performance under high concurrency.

✅ 6. Manual Synchronization (Locks)

Use ReentrantLock if you need fine-grained control.

import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class Example {
    private static final List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
    private static final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            list.add("A");
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }
}

✅ Rule of Thumb:

Concurrent updates → Use CopyOnWriteArrayList or ConcurrentLinkedQueue.

Single-thread safe removal → Use Iterator.remove().

Transformations → Use streams.

Rule of Thumb:

Few writes, many reads → CopyOnWriteArrayList

Balanced reads/writes → Collections.synchronizedList()

High concurrency / queues → ConcurrentLinkedQueue
